name: Mastodon work session - audit the account-record contract before a rework
repo: mastodon
contract_symbol: Account
contract_file: app/models/account.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 5 - a RE-QUESTION on a live anchor.
# It exists so the next phase can run the mini-bench - both arms, once, at the real wall,
# unscored - against a real baseline. Nothing here is a verdict: no delta is claimed and
# none has been measured on this shape.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION, AND THE NUMBER THAT KILLED IT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Previous yaml kept at `mastodon.yaml.names-the-class.bak` (cycle 4). Cycle 3's is at
# `mastodon.yaml.table-altitude.bak`, cycle 2's at `mastodon.yaml.line-not-file.bak`,
# cycle 1's at `mastodon.yaml.class-call-sites.bak`.
#
# Cycle 4's step 2 defined a dependent as a line that "name[s] the class itself in source"
# and then listed the five things it can do with it: call a class-level method, chain a
# query scope, read a constant, type-test against it, hand it along as a value.
#
#   MEASURED (results/loop/mastodon/minibench.md, both arms x1, exit 0, no watchdog,
#   292s and 352s of a 480s wall, both `valid: true`):
#     baseline `dependents`  24 of 24 = 1.000   (gate: at or below 0.50)  FAILS
#     sense    `dependents`  24 of 24 = 1.000
#     delta                           = 0.000   (gate: at least +0.50)    FAILS
#
# WHAT WENT WRONG WAS THE DEFINITION, NOT THE ALTITUDE. `baseline_route.py` on that run:
# seventeen tool calls, all `Bash`, fourteen of them searches. Call 4 was one token sweep,
# `grep -rn "\bAccount\b" app lib config db`, and calls 8, 9, 13, 15 and 16 were the five
# shapes the prompt had enumerated, typed back as five filters over that sweep. The ask
# defined the dependency relation as "writes this token", which is a regular expression in
# prose, and `grep -rn` prints `path:line` for free - so the citation cost the metric
# charges was never charged. Every gold row was a `both` row; `sense-only` and
# `baseline-only` were both ZERO.
#
# WHAT CHANGED THIS CYCLE (anchor kept, per laws.md ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR):
#   1. THE CALL FORM IS OUT OF SCOPE, EXPLICITLY. The ticket is a move-and-rename of the
#      class, and the ask says in as many words that the dependents which simply ask it for
#      something and get an answer back are not what is wanted. That removes the dominant,
#      most greppable form from the gold: a filtered dump of `<Class>.<method>` lines, which
#      is exactly what cycle 4's baseline handed in, scores ZERO here.
#   2. NO SHAPE IS ENUMERATED. The ask states the relation semantically - the dependency is
#      taken on the class ITSELF rather than on anything it does - and never says how such a
#      line is written. Cycle 4's five-shape list was the search strategy handed over.
#   3. GOLD IS RE-SELECTED to the thirteen rows that are dependencies of that kind, and only
#      those. Every row was opened and its line read by hand. THREE of them do not write the
#      class as a bare token at all: they write its NAME AS A STRING, so the one anchored
#      regex that covers the other ten (`[^A-Za-z_.:'"]Account[^A-Za-z_]`) misses them and a
#      second, differently-quoted search is needed to reach them.
#   4. THE ROWS THE CALL FORM REACHES MOVE TO THE NON-SCORING ANCHOR GROUP - the two
#      bare-class scope chains (`account_suggestions/source.rb`, the favourited-by listing)
#      among them. They are dependencies; they are just the kind this ticket says it is not
#      worried about, so they anchor rather than score.
#
# HONEST RISK ON THIS DRAFT, STATED AT AUTHORING TIME AND NOT A VERDICT. On this repo every
# one of the 107 app caller files `sense_blast` resolves for this contract also writes the
# literal token - checked this cycle, `comm -23` of the caller list against
# `grep -rl 'Account' app --include='*.rb'` returns EMPTY - so the sense arm's reach is a
# subset of what one token grep can print, and recall scoring cannot punish a dump. What
# this draft can still charge is the FILTER: the wanted rows are a minority of that sweep,
# no single quoting of the search covers them, and the ask no longer says which lines to
# keep. Whether that is enough is exactly what the two-arm run decides, and if a fifth
# measured baseline again sits at or above 0.50 the honest route is a swap, not a sixth
# question - this contract's dependents may simply not be grep-hostile at the line.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Account` (app/models/account.rb) is the record every other model, service, worker and
# controller in Mastodon hangs off: local users and remote actors are both rows in it. It is
# the thing a maintainer reworking identity would actually open first. Anchor UNCHANGED from
# cycle 1 - the loop does not re-scout a contract that has been measured. This is NOT a
# NO-ANCHOR: the shown blast set alone yields thirteen dependent files across ten areas.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, `Account --file app/models/account.rb`, RE-RUN this
# cycle at the current checkout, unchanged from cycles 2, 3 and 4):
#   app caller files : 107   (total incl test/non-app: 107)
#   SCATTER (dirs)   : 34
#   GREP-NOISE       : 'Account' in 257 app files   PRECISION=0.42
#   VERDICT          : SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
# Candidates profiled in earlier cycles, kept on the record so they are not re-profiled;
# none beat 0.42:
#   Account                 0.42  (107 callers / 257 grep files, 34 dirs)  <- kept
#   ActivityPub::TagManager 0.53  (75 callers / 141 grep files, 22 dirs)
#   ResolveAccountService   0.94  (16 callers / 17 grep files, 9 dirs) - near-transcription,
#     the profile laws.md records as handing a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. REJECTED.
#   AccountReachFinder      1.17  (7 callers / 6 grep files, 3 dirs) - above 1.00 means false
#     caller edges, and 7 files cannot yield twelve rows anyway. REJECTED.
#   AccountStat             RESOLVER-GAP (0 app caller files).
# Facet hunts, re-run and still dead, kept on the record:
#   - `Account::Interactions`: `sense_blast` returns ZERO direct callers.
#   - Instance-method dispatch does not resolve across app files. Re-probed this cycle over
#     MCP: `sense_graph Account#local? --direction callers` returns 12 depth-1 callers, ALL
#     inside account.rb; `Account::Suspensions#suspended?` returns 8 files, 7 of them specs;
#     `Account::Interactions#following?` returns 18 files, 17 of them specs. There is no
#     method-level facet on this contract, so no axis can be built on one.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS (the whole thesis, and what the mini-bench measures)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dependents this ticket is about do not USE the class - they depend on its IDENTITY.
# None of them asks it for anything: the class is compared against, handed to something
# generic to be dispatched on later, written down by name in a string that some other layer
# resolves back into it, or read from for a pattern or a limit it publishes for others to
# reuse. So they share no entry point AND no call: enumerating what calls the class, which
# is what every route on this repo has done for four cycles, walks straight past all
# thirteen. And because most of these files are not about the record's own concerns, its
# name recurs in them for reasons that have nothing to do with the dependency, so finding
# the file is not finding the dependency - one line is.
#
# MEASURED SUPPORT FOR THE MECHANISM, on this gold set, per row and by hand:
#   - 3 rows depend on the class's NAME AS A STRING: `'Account'` handed to a webhook worker
#     (user.rb:493), `when 'Account'` dispatching on `klass.name` (tag_manager.rb:288),
#     `target_type: 'Account'` in a polymorphic filter table (action_log_filter.rb:58). A
#     fourth string form, `class_name: 'Account'` in status.rb, is CUT as memorized (below).
#   - 6 rows read a constant the class publishes: `MENTION_RE` (extractor.rb:28,
#     account_search_service.rb:8, note_length_validator.rb:19), `USERNAME_RE`
#     (resolve_url_service.rb:7), `URL_PREFIX_RE`
#     (followers_synchronizations_controller.rb:20), `DISPLAY_NAME_LENGTH_HARD_LIMIT`
#     (process_account_service.rb:142).
#   - 4 rows compare against or hand over the class itself: `is_a?(Account)`
#     (fetch_remote_account_service.rb:7, signature_verification.rb:48), `when Account`
#     (authorize_interactions_controller.rb:11), and the class passed as an argument to a
#     generic URI resolver (activity.rb:75).
# ZERO of the thirteen is a class-level call, a scope chain, a finder or an aggregate. The
# rows that ARE those things sit in the non-scoring anchor group.
#
# WHAT A GREP PRINTS, RECORDED AND NEVER A GATE (laws.md: NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE, IN ANY
# FORM). `Account` appears in 257 app files against 107 real caller files - precision 0.42,
# the lowest profiled on this repo. The cycle-4 baseline's own anchored regex over that
# sweep covers ten of these thirteen rows and misses the three string-name rows. This is
# RECORDED; it decides nothing, and the two-arm run in the next phase is what decides.
#
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN. `sense_blast Account` is AMBIGUOUS at MCP defaults (13 symbols named
# Account; the tool returns a hint naming the `file` parameter plus top matches, so an arm
# pays one extra call to disambiguate). `sense_blast Account --file app/models/account.rb`,
# re-probed this cycle via bench/lib/mcp_probe.py at MCP defaults with no `min_confidence`,
# returns 60 shown direct-caller rows of 200 over 30 areas, risk high, truncated. All
# thirteen `dependents` files appear in that shown payload. Ten of the thirteen rows sit
# INSIDE the enclosing `def` range the payload names; THREE do not - tag_manager.rb:288
# (payload names `#uris_to_local_accounts` 250-261), action_log_filter.rb:58 (payload names
# `#scope_for` 107-122) and process_account_service.rb:142 (payload names `#call` 21-82).
# Those three are shown at FILE level: the arm is handed the file and its ref and opens it.
# Stated rather than hidden - it is a weaker guarantee than the other ten carry.
#
# MEMORIZATION (memorization_probe.py, per candidate row, run this cycle). CUT as recited:
# `Status` (recall 0.493, 34 members named - the probe's verdict is "recited, both arms
# already know it"), so `class_name: 'Account'` in status.rb is NOT gold and status.rb sits
# in the anchor group; `FeedManager` (0.727) and `InstancePresenter` (0.842), cut in earlier
# cycles and staying cut. ADMITTED: `User` 0.176 (below the bar, not recited);
# `ResolveURLService`, `NoteLengthValidator`, `AuthorizeInteractionsController`,
# `ActivityPub::Activity`, `ActivityPub::FetchRemoteAccountService` and
# `ActivityPub::FollowersSynchronizationsController` all `recall: null` (too few declared
# members to score recitation); `AccountSearchService` 0.333, `SignatureVerification` 0.375
# and `Extractor` 0.8, each labelled by the probe itself "clears 0.3 but only 2-4 member(s)
# named (< 5) - generic framework vocabulary, not recitation of THIS symbol", and each of
# those rows' credit is a line-level fact rather than a member list. `ActivityPub::TagManager`,
# `Admin::ActionLogFilter` and `ActivityPub::ProcessAccountService` carry line-level credits
# (a `when` branch on a name string, one entry in a frozen filter table, one attribute
# assignment) that no member list recites. The anchor itself comes back recited (`Account`
# 0.312) - that is the contract's own API surface, which is why the contract centre is a
# NON-SCORING anchor group; no gold row's credit is a member list.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CYCLE 6 AUTHOR - NO-ANCHOR. THIS YAML IS THE CYCLE-5 QUESTION, UNCHANGED AND UNRUN AGAIN.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing below was authored this cycle; it is the record of why a sixth question on this
# anchor was not written. Every number here is quoted command output.
#
# 1. THE ANCHOR IS RE-CONFIRMED AND RE-PROFILED, not abandoned on taste. `seam_hunt.py
#    Account --file app/models/account.rb`, re-run this cycle:
#      app caller files : 107   SCATTER (dirs) : 34
#      GREP-NOISE : 'Account' in 257 app files   PRECISION=0.42   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#    0.42 is still the lowest precision profiled on this repo (TagManager 0.53,
#    ResolveAccountService 0.94, AccountReachFinder 1.17, AccountStat resolver-gap).
#
# 2. WHAT A CREDIT COSTS HERE, read out of the scorer rather than assumed. `bench/lib/gold.py`
#    credits a gold row when the gold path is pinned to A line - `path:N`, `path (line N)`,
#    or a sibling `"line": N` - not to THE gold line. So the whole discriminator on this cell
#    is WHICH FILES an arm names; a `grep -rn` hit is already a full credit for its file.
#
# 3. THE TWO ARMS NOW PRODUCE THE SAME FILE SET. Re-read off the cycle-5 transcripts
#    (`results/claude-opus-5/minibench/*/mastodon/run-1/transcript.json`, final answers):
#      baseline answer: 74 distinct app/*.rb files pinned to lines
#      sense    answer: 75
#      sense-only files: 3   (account_policy.rb, rest/account_serializer.rb,
#                             unique_username_validator.rb)
#      baseline-only files: 2
#    Both measured baselines on this anchor scored `dependents` 1.000 (cycle 4, 24 rows;
#    cycle 5, 13 rows - cycles.jsonl). Cycle 4's gold was already the volume-and-tail shape
#    (schedulers, importers, metric dimensions, form objects) and the baseline took 24 of 24.
#
# 4. THE ARITHMETIC KILLER, RUN AGAINST THE NEXT DRAFT BEFORE WRITING IT. The bar needs a
#    baseline at or below 0.50, so a majority of any 12-row gold must be rows a token grep
#    plus one read of account.rb does not produce. The pool of such rows was probed over MCP
#    this cycle (`sense_graph Account --file app/models/account.rb --direction both`, 109
#    edges, 68 distinct files): the dependents Sense shows that do NOT write the token are
#      app/validators/unreserved_username_validator.rb
#      app/validators/empty_profile_field_names_validator.rb
#      app/validators/unique_username_validator.rb
#      app/models/account_stat.rb
#      app/models/instance.rb
#      app/models/admin/action_log.rb
#    SIX files over THREE areas, and all six are wired by a line inside account.rb itself
#    (`validates_with UniqueUsernameValidator`, the associations concern), which step 1 opens.
#    `app/policies/account_policy.rb` and `app/chewy/accounts_index.rb` - the other two
#    convention-wired candidates - are ABSENT from the shown payload, so they cannot be gold.
#    Six is below the twelve-rows-across-six-areas floor, so the framework-wiring axis (the
#    one mechanism five cycles have not asked about) cannot be golded to spec, and every
#    other row available is call-form or token-form, which is what measured 1.000 twice.
#
# 5. WHAT THIS DOES NOT SAY. It does not say mastodon is dead, and it is not derived from a
#    grep: the kills are two two-arm runs and an MCP probe of the shown pool. It says this
#    ANCHOR cannot carry a discriminating twelve-row gold under a file-level credit rule.
#    Recommendation handed up with the numbers: swap the slot for its own declared backup
#    rather than spend a seventh cycle on `Account`.
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework a class - the one whose objects are both the local
  users of this server and the remote actors it knows about. The rework changes the class
  itself: where it lives, what it is called, and what it publishes for the rest of the
  codebase to reuse. Before you touch it, audit the code that depends on it, completely
  enough that the rework cannot silently break a dependent. What counts is the exact line
  at which a dependency is taken, not the file it sits in: for each one give an exact
  file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one phrase on how it depends on the
  class. A filename with no line number does not count, and a missed dependent is a
  regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Identify the class under change
  prompt: "Task: orient. In a few sentences plus exact file:line references, name the
    single class this ticket is about, say where it is defined, and say what a rework of
    it would have to preserve. Settle on the class as a whole - the audit that follows is
    about everything in the codebase that depends on that class, not about any one part of
    it and not about how it is stored. Give file:line for everything you name.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '3'
    required: true
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the class under change
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket. Find EVERY place in the codebase that
    would have to be revisited when this class moves and is renamed, so the rework cannot
    silently break one. Be exhaustive and group them by area. Leave out the dependents that
    simply ask the class for something and get an answer back - that kind travels with the
    class, I am not worried about it, and listing it does not help me. What I need is the
    other kind: the dependency taken on the class ITSELF rather than on anything it does -
    code that depends on this being that particular class, or on something the class
    publishes for other code to reuse. Those dependents share no entry point, there is no
    method, helper or gateway they pass through, and there is no single shape to look for.
    A line that reaches the persisted form instead - the underlying table, its columns, a
    key relating one to another, or a hand-written query naming any of those - is not what
    I need here. Most of the code that depends on the class this way is not about that
    class's own concerns at all, and in those files the class's name also recurs many times
    over for reasons that have nothing to do with the dependency, so identifying the file is
    not the same as identifying the dependency: one line in it is the dependency, and that
    line is what I need. For each dependent give file:line and one phrase on how it depends
    on the class. A filename with no line number does not count, and a missed dependent is a
    regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Pinned the dependency line in each file, not just the file
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the account class. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (10, NON-SCORING anchor): the class itself, its own concerns, and the
#     call-form dependents this ticket says it is not worried about - every one of which a
#     measured arm has already reached. It does not discriminate and it is not meant to.
#   - `dependents` (13, THE discriminator): ONE row per FILE, across 10 directories. Every
#     row's file is in the shown `sense_blast` output above, and every credit below was read
#     off its own line by hand (gold_audit stamp/verify), never taken from a script tally.
#     Not one of them is a call to the class.
gold:
  # --- NON-SCORING anchors: the contract centre and the call-form dependents ---
  - {id: contract-model, group: contract, match: [models/account.rb], relation: "app/models/account.rb:65 `class Account < ApplicationRecord` - the account class itself, the contract under change"}
  - {id: c:finder-concern, group: contract, match: [account/finder_concern.rb], relation: "app/models/concerns/account/finder_concern.rb:28 `Account` heading the chain into `.remote.matches_username(...)` - the class's own lookup surface, the obvious centre both arms reach"}
  - {id: c:interactions-concern, group: contract, match: [account/interactions.rb], relation: "app/models/concerns/account/interactions.rb:123 `other_id = other_account.is_a?(Account) ? other_account.id : other_account` - the class's own concern compares its argument against the class; contract centre, reached by opening the model"}
  - {id: c:status, group: contract, match: [models/status.rb], relation: "app/models/status.rb:78 `belongs_to :in_reply_to_account, class_name: 'Account', optional: true` - the content model names the class as a string in an association; CUT from scoring as memorized (probe recall 0.493, 34 members named)"}
  - {id: c:resolve-service, group: contract, match: [services/resolve_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/resolve_account_service.rb:27 `@account ||= Account.find_remote(@username, @domain)` - the webfinger resolve path, the obvious lookup entry point; a call, so out of scope for this ticket"}
  - {id: c:account-owned-concern, group: contract, match: [account_owned_concern.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/account_owned_concern.rb:21 `@account = username_param.present? ? Account.find_local!(username_param) : Account.local.find(account_id_param)` - a call-form dependent; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 2"}
  - {id: c:json-ld-helper, group: contract, match: [helpers/json_ld_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/json_ld_helper.rb:233 `on_behalf_of ||= Account.representative` - a call-form dependent; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 2"}
  - {id: c:suggestions-source, group: contract, match: [account_suggestions/source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/source.rb:13 `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.searchable.where(discoverable: true).without_silenced.without_memorial` - a scope chain off the bare class; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 3"}
  - {id: c:friends-of-friends, group: contract, match: [friends_of_friends_source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/friends_of_friends_source.rb:10 `Account.find_by_sql([<<~SQL.squish, { id: account.id, limit: limit }])` - raw SQL run through the class; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 3"}
  - {id: c:favourited-by-accounts, group: contract, match: [statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/api/v1/statuses/favourited_by_accounts_controller.rb:22 `Account` heading a multi-line chain into `.without_suspended.includes(:favourites, :account_stat, :user)` - a scope chain off the bare class; BOTH arms cited it in cycle 4"}
  # --- THE DISCRIMINATOR: one line per file, none of them a call to the class ---
  - {id: d:user-webhook-type-name, group: dependents, match: [models/user.rb], relation: "app/models/user.rb:493 `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async('account.approved', 'Account', account_id)` - the approval path writes the class's NAME AS A STRING into a queued webhook payload for the worker to resolve back into the class"}
  - {id: d:tag-manager-klass-name, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/tag_manager.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/tag_manager.rb:288 `when 'Account'` - the generic URI-to-resource translator dispatches on `klass.name`, so it depends on the class being called exactly that"}
  - {id: d:action-log-target-type, group: dependents, match: [admin/action_log_filter.rb], relation: "app/models/admin/action_log_filter.rb:58 `memorialize_account: { target_type: 'Account', action: 'memorialize' }.freeze` - the audit-log filter table stores the class's name as the polymorphic target type it matches on"}
  - {id: d:resolve-url-username-re, group: dependents, match: [services/resolve_url_service.rb], relation: "app/services/resolve_url_service.rb:7 `USERNAME_STATUS_RE = %r{/@(?<username>#{Account::USERNAME_RE})/(statuses/)?(?<status_id>[0-9a-zA-Z]+)\\Z}` - URL resolution interpolates a username pattern the class publishes into its own route regex"}
  - {id: d:account-search-mention-re, group: dependents, match: [services/account_search_service.rb], relation: "app/services/account_search_service.rb:8 `MENTION_ONLY_RE = /\\A#{Account::MENTION_RE}\\z/i` - account search builds its exact-mention pattern out of a pattern constant the class publishes"}
  - {id: d:extractor-mention-re, group: dependents, match: [lib/extractor.rb], relation: "app/lib/extractor.rb:28 `text.scan(Account::MENTION_RE) do |screen_name, _|` - the text-entity extractor drives its mention scan off a pattern constant the class publishes"}
  - {id: d:note-length-mention-re, group: dependents, match: [validators/note_length_validator.rb], relation: "app/validators/note_length_validator.rb:19 `new_text.gsub!(Account::MENTION_RE, '@\\2')` - the profile-note length validator strips mentions with that same published pattern before counting graphemes"}
  - {id: d:followers-sync-uri-prefix, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/followers_synchronizations_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/activitypub/followers_synchronizations_controller.rb:20 `signed_request_account.uri[Account::URL_PREFIX_RE]` - follower-list synchronisation slices a partner's URI with a pattern constant the class publishes"}
  - {id: d:process-account-display-limit, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/process_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/activitypub/process_account_service.rb:142 `@account.display_name = (@json['name'] || '')[0...(Account::DISPLAY_NAME_LENGTH_HARD_LIMIT)]` - inbound actor processing truncates a remote display name to a hard limit the class publishes"}
  - {id: d:fetch-remote-actor-typecheck, group: dependents, match: [fetch_remote_account_service.rb], relation: "app/services/activitypub/fetch_remote_account_service.rb:7 `return actor if actor.nil? || actor.is_a?(Account)` - remote actor fetching compares the fetched object against the class itself and rejects anything else"}
  - {id: d:signature-verification-typecheck, group: dependents, match: [concerns/signature_verification.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/signature_verification.rb:48 `signed_request_actor.is_a?(Account) ? signed_request_actor : nil` - HTTP signature verification compares the signing actor against the class before treating it as an account"}
  - {id: d:authorize-interactions-dispatch, group: dependents, match: [authorize_interactions_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/authorize_interactions_controller.rb:11 `when Account` - the remote-follow landing page branches on the resolved resource being that class rather than a status or a collection"}
  - {id: d:activity-uri-resource, group: dependents, match: [activitypub/activity.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity.rb:75 `ActivityPub::TagManager.instance.uri_to_resource(uri, Account)` - inbound activity handling hands the class itself to a generic URI resolver as the type to resolve into"}
